    },
    "grade": "9-10",
    "points": 5,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "When you swap the digits of a two-digit code, you get the mirrored code: e.g. 80 becomes 08 and the other way around. Look at all the possible codes from 00 up to 99. How many of them differ by a perfect square from their mirrored code? (Note 0 is a perfect square as well!)",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "18"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "28"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "32"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "40"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "42"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "E",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "The difference is $9|a-b|$",
        "body": "For the code $\\overline{ab}$ the difference is $|(10a + b) - (10b + a)| = 9|a - b|$. Since $9 = 3^2$, this is a perfect square exactly when $|a - b|$ is a perfect square: $0, 1, 4$ or $9$.\n- $|a - b| = 0$: 10 codes (00, 11, …, 99)\n- $|a - b| = 1$: $9$ unordered pairs, 18 codes\n- $|a - b| = 4$: pairs $(0,4), \\ldots, (5,9)$: 6 pairs, 12 codes\n- $|a - b| = 9$: 09 and 90: 2 codes\n\nTotal $10 + 18 + 12 + 2 = 42$.\n\n**Answer: (E) 42**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "number-theory",
      "counting"
    ],
    "difficulty": 3,
