    },
    "grade": "9-10",
    "points": 5,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "Point $D$ is chosen on the side $AC$ of triangle $ABC$ so that $DC = AB$. Points $M$ and $N$ are the midpoints of the segments $AD$ and $BC$, respectively. If $\\angle NMC = \\alpha$, what is the measure of $\\angle BAC$?",
    "image": "qimages/kangaroo/ca-2017-g0910/q30.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$2\\alpha$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$90^\\circ - \\alpha$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$45^\\circ + \\alpha$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$90^\\circ - \\frac{\\alpha}{2}$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "$60^\\circ$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Midpoint of $BD$",
        "body": "Let $L$ be the midpoint of $BD$.\n- In triangle $ABD$, $ML$ joins the midpoints of $AD$ and $BD$, so $ML \\parallel AB$ and $ML = \\frac12 AB$.\n- In triangle $DBC$, $LN$ joins the midpoints of $BD$ and $BC$, so $LN \\parallel DC$ (i.e. parallel to $AC$) and $LN = \\frac12 DC$.\n\nSince $AB = DC$, we get $ML = LN$, so triangle $MLN$ is isosceles and $\\angle LMN = \\angle LNM$. Because $LN \\parallel AC$, the alternate angles give $\\angle LNM = \\angle NMC = \\alpha$. Hence $\\angle LMC = \\angle LMN + \\angle NMC = 2\\alpha$.\n\nFinally $ML \\parallel AB$, so $\\angle LMC$ (between $ML$ and $MC$) equals $\\angle BAC$ (between $AB$ and $AC$). Therefore $\\angle BAC = 2\\alpha$.\n\n**Answer: (A) $2\\alpha$**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "geometry"
    ],
    "difficulty": 3,
    "duplicateOf": null,
    "reviewNote": null
